<DocSection Anchor="fast-paging" Number="14" Title="Fast paging on large tables"
            Summary="Four things keep paging fast however big the table is. Two are automatic; two are one line each.">

    <ul class="@DocStyles.Body list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Count reuse (automatic).</strong> When only the page, the page size or the sort changes, the grid sends the total it already has (<code>KnownTotalRows</code>) and the factory skips the count. It counts again when the filters change, on <code>RefreshAsync()</code>, and when a page holds fewer rows than the total predicts.</li>
        <li><strong>Keyset paging (automatic).</strong> Moving to the next or previous page, the grid sends a cursor (<code>Cursor</code>) and the EF data source seeks from the last row seen (<code>WHERE … &gt; …</code>) instead of skipping rows (<code>OFFSET</code>), so page 5,000 costs the same as page 2. Jumping to a page number, or going back to page 1, skips as usual.</li>
        <li><strong>Projection (<code>GridFetchOptions.Projection</code>).</strong> Selects only the shown columns instead of whole entities with their includes. List any member <code>ConstructFrom</code> reads that isn't a column: <code>new GridProjection&lt;Item&gt;().Load(x =&gt; x.Notes)</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>The prerendered page (<code>PrerenderedPageKey</code>).</strong> The page fetched while prerendering is handed to the interactive grid, so each page view queries (and audits) once, and the rows are in the first HTML.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="@DocStyles.Demo">
        <MaviGrid TKey="int" FetchData="FetchPageAsync" Parameters="_parameters" PrerenderedPageKey="docs-fast-paging" />
    </div>

    <DocNote Label="What the grid sent with the last fetch">
        @if (_lastQuery is null)
        {
            <span>nothing since the page became interactive: the prerendered page was reused. Press next page.</span>
        }
        else
        {
            <span>page @_lastQuery.PageNumber, KnownTotalRows = @(_lastQuery.KnownTotalRows?.ToString() ?? "null (count)"),
                Cursor = @(_lastQuery.Cursor is { } cursor ? $"{cursor.Direction} ({cursor.Value.Length} characters)" : "null (skip rows)")</span>
        }
    </DocNote>

    <DocCode Code="@DocSnippets.FastPaging" />

    <p class="@DocStyles.Body mt-4">
        Keyset paging needs an order the database can seek: a sort on a column of the entity itself that isn't null (or
        has a null default), followed by the primary key. Otherwise the page is skipped to as before. When your own code
        loads the pages, the same hints arrive in <code>MaviGridQuery</code> (section 15).
    </p>
</DocSection>

@code {
    private static readonly GridFetchOptions<DbSampleItemOne, DocItemModel> Options = new()
    {
        Projection = new GridProjection<DbSampleItemOne>()
    };

    // Amount can't be null, so the data source can seek on (Amount, Id); a nullable sort column would be skipped to.
    private readonly MaviGridParameters _parameters = new() { SortColumnKey = "Integer" };
    private MaviGridQuery? _lastQuery;

    [Inject]
    private IGridDataFactory<DbSampleItemOne, DocItemModel, int> GridData { get; set; } = default!;

    private Task<MaviGridCollection<int>> FetchPageAsync(MaviGridQuery query, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        if (RendererInfo.IsInteractive)
        {
            _lastQuery = query;
            StateHasChanged();
        }

        return GridData.BuildPageAsync(query, Options, cancellationToken);
    }
}
